data.content:
# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: BackgroundProcessor

## Critical Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 8 — it's completely removed
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also throws** — the entire cooperative abort model is gone
3. **`ThreadAbortException` is never raised** by the runtime, so your catch block is dead code

## Why Thread.Abort Was Removed

It was inherently unsafe — it could interrupt code mid-execution (inside locks, during I/O, in `finally` blocks), corrupting state. .NET 8 enforces **cooperative cancellation** via `CancellationToken`.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<WorkItem> _channel;
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts;
    private Task? _workerTask;

    public BackgroundProcessor()
    {
        _channel = Channel.CreateBounded<WorkItem>(capacity: 100);
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
    }

    public void Start()
    {
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            // ChannelReader.ReadAllAsync respects cancellation natively —
            // no need for a volatile bool flag
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
            {
                try
                {
                    await ProcessItemAsync(item, ct);
                }
                catch (OperationCanceledException) when (ct.IsCancellationRequested)
                {
                    // Replaces ThreadAbortException catch —
                    // cooperative cancellation, no corrupted state
                    RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                    break;
                }
                finally
                {
                    await FlushBuffersAsync();
                }
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (ct.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Channel read was cancelled during idle wait — clean exit
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryComplete(); // signal no more items

        // Give worker time to finish current item gracefully
        timeout ??= TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2);

        if (_workerTask is not null)
        {
            // Wait for graceful completion, then force-cancel
            if (_workerTask != await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(timeout.Value)))
            {
                await _cts.CancelAsync(); // replaces Thread.Abort()
            }

            try
            {
                await _workerTask;
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException)
            {
                // Expected on cancellation
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Blocking `GetNextItem()`** | Replace with `Channel<T>` or async queue. A blocking call ignores `CancellationToken`, making your app hang on shutdown. |
| **`volatile bool` flag** | Insufficient — a thread blocked in `GetNextItem()` never checks the flag. `CancellationToken` interrupts the wait itself. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` in Stop** | Replace with `Task.WhenAny` + timeout. Sleep blocks the caller unconditionally. |
| **Fire-and-forget threads** | Use `Task.Run` + store the `Task` so you can `await` it during shutdown. Unobserved exceptions crash the process in .NET 8. |
| **`catch (ThreadAbortException)`** | Replace with `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (ct.IsCancellationRequested)`. The `when` guard avoids swallowing unrelated timeout exceptions. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()`** | No equivalent needed — `OperationCanceledException` is a normal exception, `finally` blocks always run. |
| **`IDisposable` on CTS** | Always dispose `CancellationTokenSource` — it holds a timer handle. Use `IAsyncDisposable`. |

## If You Must Keep Synchronous Code

If `ProcessItem` can't be made async, pass the token to check cooperatively:

```csharp
private void ProcessItem(WorkItem item, CancellationToken ct)
{
    foreach (var step in item.Steps)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // check between units of work
        ExecuteStep(step);
    }
}
```

This is the fundamental shift: the **worker checks for cancellation at safe points**, rather than the caller yanking the thread out from under it.
